#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# spot_light

#% text
# `spot_light(c, p)` is a spot light of colour `c` at the point `p`, for the option `lighting` of 3D graphics.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The 3D renderer shades faces with its own fixed light; `spot_light` is accepted in graphics and plot options, so code written for other lighting runs, and is not drawn.

#% item
# The call stays as it is when evaluated alone.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
graphics3d(sphere(), lighting: [spot_light(white, [2, 2, 2])])
#% output
#= image("spot_light.files/spot_light-1.svgz")

#% cell
plot3d(x*y, [x, -1, 1], [y, -1, 1], lighting: [spot_light(blue, [0, 0, 3])])
#% output
#= image("spot_light.files/spot_light-2.svgz")

#% cell
spot_light(white, [1, 1, 1])
#% output
#= spot_light(white, [1, 1, 1])

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The picture is the same without it:

#% cell
graphics3d([orange, sphere()])
#% output
#= image("spot_light.files/spot_light-4.svgz")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `graphics3d`, `plot3d`, `lighting`, `point_light`
